  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following congruences have *precisely four* solutions modulo $24?$\n\nI. $20x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24)$\n\nII. $16x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24)$\n\nIII. $28x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24)$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274573/assets/b65fc4830e0b060d62226b41d5ccb7bdc63d0ad594f7a42a2628597409bba3af.png)\n\n      \n\nRecall the following facts regarding the congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n):$\n\n- The congruence has no solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,b.\n$$\n\n- The congruence has $\\text{gcd}(a,n)$ solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,|\\,b.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our congruences.\n\n- Congruence I has no solutions. We have $a=20$ and $n=24$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(20,24)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=6$ is not divisible by $4,$ this congruence has no solutions.\n\n- Congruence II has eight solutions modulo $24.$ We have $a=16$ and $n=24$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(16,24)=8.\n$$\n\nSince $b=8$ is divisible by $8,$ this congruence has $8$ solutions.\n\n- Congruence III has four solutions modulo $24.$ We have $a=28$ and $n=24$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(28,24)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=8$ is divisible by $4,$ this congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
